          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure.",
          "answer": "I'm sorry to hear about your mother's diagnosis. Here is what the reference material says about heart failure.\n\nHeart failure is a long-term condition that is managed, often for many years. The name is misleading: it does not mean the heart has stopped or is about to. It means the heart cannot fill with or pump out blood as well as the body needs. (Source: *Heart failure*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe material also describes daily weight tracking. A sudden gain in weight can be an early sign that fluid is building up, so people with heart failure are often asked to weigh themselves each day and contact their care team when it changes. Many people are given a written action plan with specific thresholds, and if your mother has one, that plan is the authority to follow. (Source: *Diet and fluid in heart failure*, general clinical reference and cardiology educational summary.)\n\nThe material also says alcohol is generally limited or avoided, and that potassium supplements and salt substitutes should be discussed with her own clinician before use. (Same source.)\n\nThe search did not return anything on what symptoms need urgent attention, how to support someone with heart failure day to day, or which treatments are used. Her care team is the best source for those questions, and it may help to ask them for her written action plan and a list of warning signs to watch for.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
