          "session_id": "full-mt-m-006",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does she have to weigh herself every day?",
          "answer": "Daily weighing tracks fluid in the body. The reference material explains that weight is a proxy for fluid, and it changes faster than swelling or breathlessness does. A gain of a few pounds over two or three days, or a larger gain over a week, usually means fluid is building up rather than body tissue. Catching that early is the reason for the habit. (Source: *Diet and fluid in heart failure: sodium, volume, and the daily weight*.)\n\nThe material also says that a rise of two or three kilograms over a few days is a fluid signal and a reason to act. (Source: *Heart failure*.)\n\nTo make the numbers useful, the material suggests weighing at the same time each day, after waking and using the bathroom and before eating, and writing the result down so it can be compared across days. (Source: *Diet and fluid in heart failure*.)\n\nThe search did not say what exact weight change her care team wants reported, so her written action plan or her care team should give those thresholds.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
